  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1255 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nUpper ridging will take hold of the region late weekend into early \nnext week and will give a lull in precip chances while also warming \nafternoon highs into the mid 80s to mid 90s Sunday and Monday. \nUnlike just a couple of weeks ago, warm temps will only be \ntemporary. An upper shortwave trough is expected to break from an \nupper low currently over the Bering Sea and move into the Pacific \nCoast as a closed low by late Sunday. This closed low will deepen as \nit moves southeastward into the Desert Southwest Monday and Tuesday. \nDuring this time, the upper low will begin to siphon moisture from \nHurricane Polo in the eastern Pacific with Polo eventually phasing \nwith the upper low. This will greatly enhance moisture availability \nas the upper low begins moving eastward across Arizona and New Mexico \nby mid next week. Depending on the speed and timing of the upper \nlow, this could result in widespread heavy rainfall for much of the \nregion Wednesday and Thursday. This forecast is still too far out to \nbegin highlighting hydro concerns, and there is still a fair amount \nof uncertainty with the timing and location of the upper low. It \ndoes, however, bare watching for now. Another factor in the forecast \nwould be the potential for a cold front during this time frame which \nwould greatly enhance lift. What is more certain is the continuance \nof fall like temperatures through much of mid to late next week \nthanks to this system.",
